\fs48 \cf2 Conditional expressions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo also supports conditional expressions (or what is sometimes called a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ternary_conditional_operator"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 ternary conditional operator}}) using the syntax
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 true_result if boolean_expression else false_result
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , just as in Python. This is most often used as a concise way to assign one of two different values to a variable, based on a boolean condition.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 temp_celsius \cf5 =\cf13  \cf15 15\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14 forecast \cf5 =\cf13  \cf16 "warm"\cf13  \cf5 if\cf13  temp_celsius \cf5 >\cf13  \cf15 20\cf13  \cf5 else\cf13  \cf16 "cool"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf16 "The forecast for today is"\cf5 ,\cf13  forecast\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 The forecast for today is cool\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The alternative, written as a multi-line 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement, is more verbose.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 if\cf13  temp_celsius \cf5 >\cf13  \cf15 20\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     forecast \cf5 =\cf13  \cf16 "warm"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     forecast \cf5 =\cf13  \cf16 "cool"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf16 "The forecast for today is"\cf5 ,\cf13  forecast\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
