\fs64 \cf2 The 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  statement\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop iterates over a sequence, executing a code block for each element in the sequence. The Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop can iterate over any type that implements an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __iter__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that returns a type that defines 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __next__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __len__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Iterating over Mojo collections\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 All of the collection types in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 collections}} module support 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop iteration. See the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/types/#collection-types"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Collection types}} documentation for more information on Mojo collection types.\
The following shows an example of iterating over a Mojo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/list/List/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 List}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 states \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf16 "California"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf16 "Hawaii"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf16 "Oregon"\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  state \cf5 in\cf13  states\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 state\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 California\cb1 \
\cb14 Hawaii\cb1 \
\cb14 Oregon\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The same technique works for iterating over a Mojo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/set/Set/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Set}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 collections \cf5 import\cf13  Set\cb1 \
\
\cb14 values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 \{\cf15 42\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 0\cf5 \}\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  item \cf5 in\cf13  values\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 item\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 42\cb1 \
\cb14 0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are two techniques for iterating over a Mojo {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/Dict/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 Dict}}. The first is to iterate directly using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which produces a sequence of the dictionary's keys.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  capitals\cf5 :\cf13  Dict\cf5 [\cf13 String\cf5 ,\cf13  String\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 \{\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf16 "California"\cf5 :\cf13  \cf16 "Sacramento"\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf16 "Hawaii"\cf5 :\cf13  \cf16 "Honolulu"\cf5 ,\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf16 "Oregon"\cf5 :\cf13  \cf16 "Salem"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 \}\cf13 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  \cf5 var\cf13  state \cf5 in\cf13  capitals\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 capitals\cf5 [\cf13 state\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  \cf16 ", "\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  state\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Sacramento, California\cb1 \
\cb14 Honolulu, Hawaii\cb1 \
\cb14 Salem, Oregon\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The second approach to iterating over a Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Dict
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is to invoke its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/Dict/#items"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 items()}} method, which produces a sequence of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/collections/dict/DictEntry/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 DictEntry}} objects. Within the loop body, you can then access the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 key
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 value
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  fields of the entry.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  item \cf5 in\cf13  capitals\cf5 .\cf13 items\cf5 ():\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 item\cf5 .\cf13 value \cf5 +\cf13  \cf16 ", "\cf13  \cf5 +\cf13  item\cf5 .\cf13 key\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Sacramento, California\cb1 \
\cb14 Honolulu, Hawaii\cb1 \
\cb14 Salem, Oregon\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Iterating using references\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo collection iterators all return {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/lifetimes/#working-with-references"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 references}}, which are captured immutably into the loop variable. If you'd like to get a reference to a mutable element, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ref
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword in front of the loop variable to create a reference binding that matches the element reference.\
This can be useful if you want to mutate the value in the collection:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  values \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 4\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 7\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 6\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 11\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  \cf5 ref\cf13  value \cf5 in\cf13  values\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  value \cf5 %\cf13  \cf15 2\cf13  \cf5 !=\cf13  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         value \cf5 -=\cf13  \cf15 1\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf13 values\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 [0, 4, 6, 2, 6, 10]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Iterating ranges\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Another type of iterable provided by the Mojo standard library is a range, which is a sequence of integers generated by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/range/range/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 range()}} function. It differs from the collection types shown above in that it's implemented as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generator_(computer_programming)"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 generator}}, producing each value as needed rather than materializing the entire sequence in memory. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf21 range\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 i\cf5 ,\cf13  end\cf5 =\cf16 ", "\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 0, 1, 2, 3, 4,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  loop control statements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 continue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement skips execution of the rest of the code block and resumes the loop with the next element of the collection.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf21 range\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  i \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 continue\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 i\cf5 ,\cf13  end\cf5 =\cf16 ", "\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 0, 1, 2, 4,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 break
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statement terminates execution of the loop.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf21 range\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  i \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf15 3\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 break\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 i\cf5 ,\cf13  end\cf5 =\cf16 ", "\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 0, 1, 2,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Optionally, a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop can include an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause. The body of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause executes after iterating over all of the elements in a collection.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  \cf21 range\cf5 (\cf15 5\cf5 ):\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 i\cf5 ,\cf13  end\cf5 =\cf16 ", "\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf16 "\\nFinished executing 'for' loop"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 0, 1, 2, 3, 4,\cb1 \
\cb14 Finished executing 'for' loop\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 else
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause executes even if the collection is empty.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 collections \cf5 import\cf13  List\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf13  empty\cf5 :\cf13  List\cf5 [\cf13 Int\cf5 ]\cf13  \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 []\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  i \cf5 in\cf13  empty\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf13 i\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Finished executing 'for' loop"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Finished executing 'for' loop\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb19 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 else
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  clause does not execute if a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 break
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  or 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 return
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  statement terminates the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 for
\fs28 \cf2 \cb19  loop.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 collections \cf5 import\cf13  List\cb1 \
\
\cb14 animals \cf5 =\cf13  \cf5 [\cf16 "cat"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf16 "aardvark"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf16 "hippopotamus"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf16 "dog"\cf5 ]\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 for\cf13  animal \cf5 in\cf13  animals\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf13  animal \cf5 ==\cf13  \cf16 "dog"\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf16 "Found a dog"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 break\cf13 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf16 "No dog found"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 Found a dog\cb1 \
