\fs48 \cf2 Iterating over Python collections\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 for
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  loop supports iterating over Python collection types. Each item retrieved by the loop is a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 PythonObject}} wrapper around the Python object. Refer to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/python/types/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Python types}} documentation for more information on manipulating Python objects from Mojo.\
The following is a simple example of iterating over a mixed-type Python list.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 python \cf5 import\cf13  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf21 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Create a mixed-type Python list\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     py_list \cf5 =\cf13  Python\cf5 .\cf21 list\cf5 (\cf15 42\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf16 "cat"\cf5 ,\cf13  \cf15 3.14159\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 for\cf13  py_obj \cf5 in\cf13  py_list\cf5 :\cf13   \cf6 # Each element is of type "PythonObject"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf13 py_obj\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 42\cb1 \
\cb14 cat\cb1 \
\cb14 3.14159\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There are two techniques for iterating over a Python dictionary. The first is to iterate directly using the dictionary, which produces a sequence of its keys.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 python \cf5 import\cf13  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf21 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Create a mixed-type Python dictionary\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     py_dict \cf5 =\cf13  Python\cf5 .\cf13 evaluate\cf5 (\cf16 "\{'a': 1, 'b': 2.71828, 'c': 'sushi'\}"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 for\cf13  py_key \cf5 in\cf13  py_dict\cf5 :\cf13   \cf6 # Each key is of type "PythonObject"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf13 py_key\cf5 ,\cf13  py_dict\cf5 [\cf13 py_key\cf5 ])\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 a 1\cb1 \
\cb14 b 2.71828\cb1 \
\cb14 c sushi\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The second approach to iterating over a Python dictionary is to invoke its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 items()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method, which produces a sequence of 2-tuple objects. Within the loop body, you can then access the key and value by index.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 from\cf13  std\cf5 .\cf13 python \cf5 import\cf13  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 def\cf13  \cf21 main\cf5 ()\cf13  raises\cf5 :\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf6 # Create a mixed-type Python dictionary\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     py_dict \cf5 =\cf13  Python\cf5 .\cf13 evaluate\cf5 (\cf16 "\{'a': 1, 'b': 2.71828, 'c': 'sushi'\}"\cf5 )\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 for\cf13  py_tuple \cf5 in\cf13  py_dict\cf5 .\cf13 items\cf5 ():\cf13   \cf6 # Each 2-tuple is of type "PythonObject"\cf13 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf13 py_tuple\cf5 [\cf15 0\cf5 ],\cf13  py_tuple\cf5 [\cf15 1\cf5 ])\cf13 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf13 \cb14 a 1\cb1 \
\cb14 b 2.71828\cb1 \
\cb14 c sushi\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0

